A pip.parse for a Python version that has a toolchain but no interpreter for
this host fails the whole pip extension.  grpc 1.76.0 calls pip.parse for
3.9 through 3.13, and rules_python ships no 3.9 or 3.10 CPython for
aarch64-pc-windows-msvc, so on Windows ARM64 the extension fails and takes
bazel's own 3.11 hub down with it.

Skip such a version the way a version missing from minor_mapping is already
skipped.  This is the change proposed upstream, back-ported to 1.9.2.  CI only.

--- a/python/private/pypi/hub_builder.bzl	2026-09-26 20:38:31
+++ b/python/private/pypi/hub_builder.bzl	2026-09-26 20:38:51
@@ -174,6 +174,25 @@
             "Ignoring pip python version '{version}' for hub " +
             "'{hub}' in module '{module}' because there is no registered " +
             "toolchain for it."
+        ).format(
+            hub = self.name,
+            module = self.module_name,
+            version = python_version,
+        ))
+        return
+
+    if (pip_attr.python_interpreter_target == None and
+        not pip_attr.python_interpreter and
+        _host_interpreter_name(pip_attr.python_version) not in self._available_interpreters):
+        # The version has a toolchain, but none of its interpreters runs on
+        # this host, e.g. there is no CPython build for this os and cpu. Treat
+        # it like a version missing from minor_mapping instead of failing the
+        # extension, which would also take down every other version and hub,
+        # including ones this host can use.
+        self._logger.info(lambda: (
+            "Ignoring pip python version '{version}' for hub " +
+            "'{hub}' in module '{module}' because there is no " +
+            "interpreter for it that runs on this host."
         ).format(
             hub = self.name,
             module = self.module_name,
@@ -409,13 +428,14 @@
         parallel_download = pip_attr.parallel_download,
     )
     return True
